1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"give-a-way-back-in","number":"09","title":"Restore the argument at each stop.","mode":"No re-entry","scale":"talk","dek":"At each section break, restore the economic problem the next section solves.","before":[],"after":[],"diagnosis":"The dividers name the next section without restoring the argument. A listener returning after several technical slides still has to recover why the solution, calibration, or experiment matters.","changes":["Keep the original sequence and its early result preview.","Replace each bare divider with a recap and one fact to carry forward.","Say what the next section will answer."],"preserved":"The source’s sequence, model, calibration, policy experiment, and fiscal qualification. The outline groups the progressive decomposition on pp. 21–26 into one stage.","deferred":"Solver and calibration details stay in their sections. Supporting pages 34–41 remain supporting material.","practice":"After each technical block, write two sentences for someone returning to the room: what we have, and why the next step matters.","principle":"Give people a way back in","sourceType":"public","context":"Warwick presentation. PDF p. 4 already previews the main finding. This repair concerns the visible transitions at pp. 10, 14, and 19; pp. 18–20 move from external lottery evidence through the Results divider to a model policy experiment.","beforeCaption":"Three source dividers, shown in their original order.","afterCaption":"","outline":{"title":"Restore the argument at each stop","description":"A 41-page research deck, with builds grouped and supporting slides separated. Keep the order and replace bare section dividers with an account of what the audience should carry forward.","before":[{"label":"Source section","title":"The transmission question","content":"Lower interest rates affect spending directly. They also change earnings, asset returns, and transfers.","pages":"2–5","role":"setup","shortTitle":"The transmission question","id":"hank-question","takeaway":"Liquid returns · Labor income · Illiquid returns · Transfers"},{"label":"Source section","title":"The household model","content":"The liquid asset pays for current spending. The illiquid asset earns a higher equilibrium return but is costly to adjust.","pages":"6–9","role":"setup","shortTitle":"The household model","id":"hank-households","takeaway":"\\(r^a>r^b\\) · Asset transfers carry an adjustment cost."},{"label":"Source section","title":"Solution methods","content":"Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman and Kolmogorov forward equations; equilibrium computation.","pages":"10–13","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Solution methods","panels":[{"kind":"divider","title":"Solution Method","sourcePage":"10","id":"hank-solution-divider"},{"kind":"flow","title":"Solution Method","label":"SOLVING THE ECONOMY","items":[{"title":"Household choices","text":"The Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation determines spending and asset transfers."},{"title":"Wealth distribution","text":"The Kolmogorov forward equation tracks the resulting distribution of households."}],"note":"Prices make these choices consistent with market clearing.","id":"hank-solution","provenance":"Source content · schematic","sourcePage":"9, 11"}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Parameterization","content":"Income process, wealth distribution, marginal propensities to consume, and external spending evidence.","pages":"14–18","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Parameterization","panels":[{"kind":"divider","title":"Parameterization","sourcePage":"14","id":"hank-calibration-divider"},{"kind":"hank-wealth","title":"Model and data: wealth distribution","label":"HOUSEHOLD PORTFOLIOS","note":"Average quarterly MPC from a $500 windfall in the model: 16%.","id":"hank-calibration","provenance":"Source content · schematic","sourcePage":"16–17"}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Results","content":"Monetary shock, impulse responses, and direct versus indirect channels.","pages":"19–28","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Results","panels":[{"kind":"divider","title":"Results","sourcePage":"19","id":"hank-shock-divider"},{"kind":"equation","title":"Transmission of a monetary policy shock","label":"THE MONETARY POLICY EXPERIMENT","formula":"i_t=\\bar r^b+\\phi\\pi_t+\\varepsilon_t","lines":["Initial shock: \\(\\varepsilon_0=-0.0025\\), or −1 percentage point annualized.","Direct channel: liquid return \\(r^b\\).","Indirect channels: illiquid return \\(r^a\\), wage \\(w\\), transfers \\(T\\)."],"note":"\\(i_t\\): nominal rate · \\(\\pi_t\\): inflation · \\(\\phi\\): policy response","id":"hank-shock","provenance":"Source content · schematic","sourcePage":"20, 26"}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Alternative fiscal responses","content":"Transfers, government purchases, public debt, shock persistence, and alternative household benchmarks.","pages":"29–32","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Alternative fiscal responses","panels":[{"kind":"hank-fiscal","title":"Role of the fiscal response","label":"THREE FISCAL RESPONSES","note":"Initial consumption elasticity with respect to the liquid return; associated direct-effect share.","id":"hank-fiscal","provenance":"Source content · schematic","sourcePage":"29"}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Conclusion and supporting results","content":"HANK and RANK transmission summaries; additional wealth, consumption, and counterfactual results.","pages":"33; support 34–41","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Conclusion and supporting results","panels":[{"kind":"hank-transmission","title":"Monetary transmission in RANK and HANK","label":"TRANSMISSION SUMMARY","id":"hank-conclusion","provenance":"Source content · schematic","sourcePage":"33"}]}],"after":[{"title":"How does a rate cut reach household spending?","shortTitle":"The transmission question","content":"Lower interest rates affect spending directly. They also change earnings, asset returns, and transfers.","pages":"2–5","takeaway":"Liquid returns · Labor income · Illiquid returns · Transfers","label":"","role":"setup","revises":["hank-question"]},{"title":"Liquid and illiquid assets support different spending choices.","shortTitle":"Portfolios and markets","content":"The liquid asset pays for current spending. The illiquid asset earns a higher equilibrium return but is costly to adjust.","pages":"6–9","takeaway":"\\(r^a>r^b\\) · Asset transfers carry an adjustment cost.","label":"","role":"setup","revises":["hank-households"]},{"title":"Household choices and the wealth distribution must agree.","shortTitle":"Joint solution","content":"Household choices and their distribution are solved together, with prices consistent with market clearing.","pages":"10–13","panels":[{"kind":"flow","title":"Household choices and the wealth distribution must agree.","label":"SOLVING THE ECONOMY","items":[{"title":"Household choices","text":"The Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation determines spending and asset transfers."},{"title":"Wealth distribution","text":"The Kolmogorov forward equation tracks the resulting distribution of households."}],"note":"Prices make these choices consistent with market clearing.","revises":["hank-solution","hank-solution-divider"]}],"role":"bridge","label":""},{"title":"Does the model reproduce household wealth patterns?","shortTitle":"Wealth and spending","content":"Household data discipline portfolios and spending responses.","pages":"14–18","panels":[{"kind":"hank-wealth","title":"Does the model match household wealth?","label":"HOUSEHOLD PORTFOLIOS","note":"Average quarterly MPC from a $500 windfall in the model: 16%.","revises":["hank-calibration","hank-calibration-divider"]}],"role":"bridge","label":""},{"title":"The rate cut changes returns, earnings, and transfers.","shortTitle":"The rate-cut experiment","content":"The policy experiment exposes heterogeneous households to the same monetary shock.","pages":"19–28","panels":[{"kind":"equation","title":"The rate cut changes returns, earnings, and transfers.","label":"THE MONETARY POLICY EXPERIMENT","formula":"i_t=\\bar r^b+\\phi\\pi_t+\\varepsilon_t","lines":["Initial shock: \\(\\varepsilon_0=-0.0025\\), or −1 percentage point annualized.","Direct channel: liquid return \\(r^b\\).","Indirect channels: illiquid return \\(r^a\\), wage \\(w\\), transfers \\(T\\)."],"note":"\\(i_t\\): nominal rate · \\(\\pi_t\\): inflation · \\(\\phi\\): policy response","revises":["hank-shock","hank-shock-divider"]}],"role":"bridge","label":""},{"title":"Fiscal adjustment changes monetary transmission.","shortTitle":"The fiscal response","content":"Lower interest payments finance different combinations of transfers, purchases, or debt adjustment.","pages":"29–32","panels":[{"kind":"hank-fiscal","title":"Fiscal adjustment changes monetary transmission.","label":"THREE FISCAL RESPONSES","note":"Initial consumption elasticity with respect to the liquid return; associated direct-effect share.","revises":["hank-fiscal"]}],"label":"","role":"setup"},{"title":"Indirect channels account for most of the HANK spending response.","shortTitle":"The transmission mechanism","content":"Wealthy households’ income effects offset part of intertemporal substitution. Hand-to-mouth households make spending sensitive to income.","pages":"33; support 34–41","panels":[{"kind":"hank-transmission","title":"Indirect channels account for most of the HANK response.","label":"TRANSMISSION SUMMARY","revises":["hank-conclusion"]}],"label":"","role":"setup"}],"change":"Keep the source sequence and replace bare section dividers with a recap, a carry-forward fact, and the next question.","overviewTitle":"Household portfolios shape monetary transmission."},"source":{"authors":"Greg Kaplan, Benjamin Moll, Gianluca Violante","title":"Monetary Policy According to HANK","date":"11 May 2017","url":"https://benjaminmoll.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/HANK_slides.pdf","pages":"selected slides from 2–41"},"values":["Wealth moments: data/model illiquid assets-to-GDP 2.920/2.920; liquid assets-to-GDP 0.260/0.263; poor hand-to-mouth 10%/10%; wealthy hand-to-mouth 20%/19%. PDF p.16.","Fiscal response table, PDF p.29: transfers, purchases, debt give consumption elasticities −2.21, −2.07, −1.48 and direct-effect shares 19%, 22%, 46%.","The concluding direct/indirect decomposition is the summary on PDF p.33. It is distinct from the three fiscal scenarios on p.29."],"topic":"Monetary policy","point":"The same model, calibration, and experiment appear in both outlines. The revision turns three bare section dividers into transitions that restate the economic question."}}]
